In <199309161949.AA15829@eff.org> mc/G=Brad/S=Hicks/OU=0205925@mhs.attmail.com writes:

>The clear intent here is to "chill" expression by making everybody err on
>the side of caution, so that no only the objectionable stuff (a separate
>debate, to be sure) but also anything that you might be afraid =might= be
>objectionable has to be destroyed.
> 
>In US obscenity law, there's a couple of common escape clauses: you can't
>convict if it fails to be found objectionable by "contemporary community
>standards" as judged by a jury, not a central self-selected board of
>censors, and you can't convict if, according to a preponderance of expert
>testimony, the work/item has =any= "serious" scientific, literary,
>artistic, educational, or medical merit.
> 
>Does EnZed law have either of these escape clauses, or have you actually
>managed to enact an even more repressive obscenity standard than ours?

The definition of what's obscene is:

  3. Meaning of "objectionable"--(1) For the purposes of this Act, a 
     publication is objectionable if it describes, depicts, expresses, or 
     otherwise deals with matters of sex, horror, crime, cruelty, or 
     violence in such a manner that the availability of the publication is 
     likely to be injurious to the public good. 
 
     (2) A publication shall be deemed to be objectionable for the 
         purposes of this Act if the publication promotes or supports, or 
         tends to promote or support,--
 
         (a) The exploitation of children, or young persons, or both, for 
             sexual purposes; or       
         (b) The use of violence or coercion to compel any person to 
             participate in, or submit to, sexual conduct; or 
         (c) Sexual conduct with or upon the body of a dead person; or
         (d) The use of urine or excrement in association with degrading 
             or dehumanising conduct or sexual conduct; or 
         (e) Beastiality; or
         (f) Acts of torture or the infliction of extreme violence or 
             extreme cruelty. 

  (3) In determining, for the purposes of this Act, whether or not any 
      publication (other than a publication to which subsection (2) of this 
      section applies) is objectionable or should be given a classification 
      other than objectionable, particular weight shall be given to the 
      extent and degree to which, and the manner in which, the publication--
    
      (a) Describes, depicts, or otherwise deals with--
        
          (i)   Acts of torture, the infliction of serious physical harm, 
                or acts of significant cruelty: 
          (ii)  Sexual violence or sexual coercion, or violence or 
                coercion in association with sexual conduct: 
          (iii) Other sexual or physical conduct of a degrading or 
                dehumanising nature: 
          (iv)  Sexual conduct with or by children, or young persons, 
                or both: 
          (v)   Physical conduct in which sexual satisfaction is derived 
                from inflicting or suffering cruelty or pain: 
    
      (b) Exploits the nudity of children, or young persons, or both:
 
      (c) Degrades or dehumanises any person:
 
      (d) Promotes or encourages criminal acts or acts of terrorism:
 
      (e) Represents (whether directly or by implication) that members of
          of any particular class of the public are inherently inferior to 
          other members of the public by reason of any characteristic of that
          class, being a characteristic that is a prohibited ground of 
          discrimination specified in section 21 (1) of the Human Rights Act 
          1993.

  4. Whether publication objectionable a matter of expert judgement--
     The question whether or not a publication is objectionable is a matter 
     for the expert judgement of the person or body authorised or required, 
     by or pursuant to this Act, to determine it, and evidence as to, or 
     proof of, any of the matters or particulars that the person or body is 
     required to consider in determining that question is not essential to 
     its determination. 

In other words the authorised person or body can rule something objectionable
without having to give any reasons why - great material for backing up any
witch-hunt someone feels like conducting.

Finally, here are the defences you're allowed:

 (5) It *is* a defence to a charge under subsection 1 of this section if the 
     defendant *proves* that the defendant had possession of the publication 
     to which the charge relates, in good faith,---
   
     (a) [ when handing it in to the police/censors ]
 
     (b) for the purposes of any proceedings under this act
 
     (c) [ when legally defending someone who's charged under this act ]
 
     (d) when complying to any orders by the Chief Censor, Judges, etc
 
     (e) in connection with the delivery of the publication to the National 
         Librarian in accordance with section 64 of the Copyright Act 1962.

Note the lack of a "The defendant didn't even know the material existed"
clause....

